WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:09.367
Git push. A buildpack turns the repository into something runnable. A router gives it a name.

2
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:20.112
That is the whole promise of platform as a service, and this episode asks what it does for a microservice — and what it will never draw.

3
00:00:20.712 --> 00:00:33.574
NIST draws the line in 2011: the consumer does not manage the network, the servers, the operating system, or the storage; the consumer does control the deployed applications.

4
00:00:33.574 --> 00:00:43.680
NIST’s evaluation guide names who that consumer is — the developer or deployer, not the end user, not the operator of machines.

5
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:52.408
So a PaaS sits between the two models we already have. Infrastructure as a service hands the team machines.

6
00:00:52.408 --> 00:00:57.001
Software as a service hands a tenant a finished product.

7
00:00:57.001 --> 00:01:07.566
Platform as a service hands a service team a place to run one service, and the next service, without handing them the machines.

8
00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:17.672
Heroku is the classic public example; Cloud Foundry, from 2011, the classic private one; the Twelve-Factor App is the contract both expect.

9
00:01:18.272 --> 00:01:27.840
On Heroku the unit is an app. A Procfile names process types: a web process takes HTTP; a worker consumes a queue.

10
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:35.668
Each running copy is a dyno — one command, in a container, disposable. Configuration sits in the environment.

11
00:01:35.668 --> 00:01:40.886
A database or a queue is an add-on, bound to the app.

12
00:01:40.886 --> 00:01:54.803
From the architecture’s point of view, one bounded context is usually one app, with its web process and its worker as two process types of the same service — not two microservices.

13
00:01:54.803 --> 00:02:03.066
A second context is a second app. And the platform will happily bind both apps to one shared database.

14
00:02:03.066 --> 00:02:11.763
Database-per-service remains a decision in the repositories and in the bindings the teams choose, not a rule of the dyno.

15
00:02:12.363 --> 00:02:21.617
The PaaS is Richardson’s service deployment platform as a product. Instance per container, hidden. Externalized configuration, as bindings.

16
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.188
Backing services as attached resources.

17
00:02:24.188 --> 00:02:38.583
Server-side discovery through the router — the caller never picks an instance, and the price of that simplicity is that the team also does not see the instance.

18
00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:49.894
Disposability: state belongs in the bound store, and logs go to the platform’s stream. Scaling on the X axis, by adding dynos.

19
00:02:49.894 --> 00:02:54.521
Third-party registration, by the platform that started the process.

20
00:02:55.121 --> 00:02:57.961
A PaaS app is a release unit.

21
00:02:57.961 --> 00:03:06.076
It becomes a microservice only if it owns its data and can be released without the apps next to it.

22
00:03:06.076 --> 00:03:13.785
Twenty apps that share one schema and one release train are a distributed monolith that happens to push cleanly.

23
00:03:13.785 --> 00:03:20.277
Nothing in the push model implements a saga, an outbox, a tenant predicate, or an aggregate.

24
00:03:20.277 --> 00:03:28.797
If a message has no tenant, the SaaS requirement is already lost, and the metering of dyno-hours will not catch it.

25
00:03:28.797 --> 00:03:32.854
The platform meters compute. The service has to meter meaning.

26
00:03:33.454 --> 00:03:37.264
The opinion of the platform is a real constraint.

27
00:03:37.264 --> 00:03:46.576
Sidecars, a mesh, and fine network policy are awkward on a classic PaaS, because those knobs were the thing the platform hid.

28
00:03:46.576 --> 00:03:57.158
Teams that need them step down to Kubernetes, where those objects are first-class — a change in the responsibility line, not in the bounded context.

29
00:03:57.158 --> 00:04:03.507
Many current platforms run on a cluster and show the team only the narrow door.

30
00:04:03.507 --> 00:04:15.782
And a platform team, in the Team Topologies sense, is an internal PaaS even when the substrate is Kubernetes: a pipeline, a build, a bind, a route, a dashboard.

31
00:04:16.382 --> 00:04:26.003
A company can build its SaaS product by placing each service on a PaaS. The PaaS control plane onboards applications and dynos.

32
00:04:26.003 --> 00:04:34.312
The SaaS control plane onboards tenants and tiers. They share a word and they do not share a job.

33
00:04:34.312 --> 00:04:36.061
Dynos are not tenants.

34
00:04:36.061 --> 00:04:45.244
So an AI claim about PaaS has to show a stateless process, configuration outside the build, and backing services as bindings.

35
00:04:45.244 --> 00:04:54.864
It can be a correct twelve-factor app and still share a database, omit the tenant, and call five neighbors in one request.

36
00:04:54.864 --> 00:04:58.800
Pushing to a platform discharges neither claim by itself.

37
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:09.649
Three things to carry away. One. A PaaS runs the process you push; the meaning of the process is still yours. Two.

38
00:05:09.649 --> 00:05:18.034
A push-clean app can still share a database, omit the tenant, and call five neighbors per request. Three.

39
00:05:18.034 --> 00:05:26.886
Dynos are not tenants; the PaaS control plane and the SaaS control plane share a word, not a job.

40
00:05:27.486 --> 00:05:33.474
Next: what a system owes when it is sold as software as a service — tenant by tenant.
